%S A075861 1,1,2,3,2,5,4,3,5,9,4,11,7,5,8,15,6,17,10,7,11,21,8,15,13,9,14,27,10,
%T A075861 29,16,11,17,21,12,35,19,13,20,39,14,41,22,15,23,45,16,35,25,17,26,51,
%U A075861 18,33,28,19,29,57,20,59,31,21,32,39,22,65,34,23,35,69,24,71,37,25,38
%N A075861 Least k such that (n-k) divides (n+k).
%C A075861 a(n) is also the least k>0 such that F(n-k) divides F(n+k), where F = A000045 (Fibonacci numbers).  More generally, if (f(n)) is a divisibility sequence (that is, f(k)|f(n) if and only k|n), then a(n) is the least k>0 such that f(n-k) divides f(n+k).  More examples of such f(n):  2^n-1, 3^n-1, n^2, n^3. - _Clark Kimberling_, Jul 30 2012
%C A075861 From _Benoit Cloitre_, Oct 23 2026: (Start)
%C A075861 Proof of the exact formulas below: putting d = n-k, the condition becomes d | 2*n with d < n. Minimizing k therefore amounts to finding the largest divisor of 2*n < n, or equivalently its least complementary divisor greater than 2.
%C A075861 For the asymptotic formula, integers with smallest prime factor p have density delta_p = (1/p)*Product_{q prime, q < p}(1 - 1/q). Thus Sum_{n=2..N} n/p(n) ~ (N^2/2)*Sum_{p prime} delta_p/p. Truncation is justified because the contribution from p(n) > y is at most N*(N+1)/(2*y). Summing the identity below gives c.
%C A075861 (End)
%H A075861 Amiram Eldar, <a href="/A075861/b075861.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 2..10000</a>
%H A075861 Vaclav Kotesovec, <a href="/A075861/a075861.jpg">Plot of Sum_{k=1..n} a(k)/n^2 for n = 1..10000</a>
%F A075861 From _Benoit Cloitre_, Oct 23 2026: (Start)
%F A075861 a(n) = n - 2*n/min{d > 2 : d divides 2*n}.
%F A075861 For odd n, a(n) = n - 2*n/p(n), where p(n) is the smallest prime factor of n. For even n, a(n) = n/3 if 3 divides n, and a(n) = n/2 otherwise.
%F A075861 Equivalently, a(n) = n - 2*n/p(n) + (n/2)*[2 divides n] - (n/6)*[6 divides n], where [P] equals 1 if P is true and 0 otherwise.
%F A075861 Sum_{n=2..N} a(n) ~ c*N^2, where c = 11/18 - Sum_{p prime} (1/p^2)*Product_{q prime, q < p}(1 - 1/q) = 0.28101307....
%F A075861 liminf_{n->infinity} a(n)/n = 1/3; limsup_{n->infinity} a(n)/n = 1.
%F A075861 (End)
%t A075861 Table[i=1;While[!Divisible[n+i,n-i],i++];i,{n,2,100}]  (* _Harvey P. Dale_, Mar 28 2011 *)
%o A075861 (PARI) a(n)=if(n<0,0,s=1; while((n+s)%(n-s)>0,s++); s)
%K A075861 nonn,changed
%O A075861 2,3
%A A075861 _Benoit Cloitre_, Oct 15 2002
